+# Combination: vectorized join + datom segments
+
+Branch `exp/combo-vec-seg`. Merges two finished experiments and measures whether their gains
+compose:
+
+- **`exp/vectorized-join`** — columnar (batched) generic join. `TRIPLOX_BATCHED_JOIN=1`.
+  Attacks the *row bookkeeping*: one `Vec<Bytes>` per intermediate row becomes shared columns
+  plus a `Vec<u32>` row map.
+- **`exp/datom-segments`** — many datom keys packed into one SlateDB entry.
+  `TRIPLOX_SEGMENT_SIZE=256`. Attacks the *storage access*: 45953 LSM keys per index become 224.
+
+Design notes for each are kept verbatim in `docs/vectorized-join-EXPERIMENT.md`,
+`docs/datom-segments-EXPERIMENT.md` (and the two `-PROGRESS.md` files).
+
+## What was merged, and the conflicts
+
+Merge order: `exp/vectorized-join`, then `exp/datom-segments`. Conflicts were trivial.
+
+| file | conflict | resolution |
+|---|---|---|
+| `EXPERIMENT.md`, `PROGRESS.md` | add/add, both merges | source-branch copies moved to `docs/<branch>-*.md`; this worktree's `PROGRESS.md` kept |
+| `src/query/patterns/triple.rs` | import block only | kept both `query::vectorized::{batch,...}` and `segment` imports |
+
+Everything else auto-merged. That is not luck — the two features meet at exactly two places and
+both already had the right shape:
+
+1. **Reads.** The batched path (`grouped_extensions`, `validate_grouped`, `scan_all`) goes through
+   `Box<dyn Index>` — `SlateIterator` / `TemporalFilterIterator` — and datom-segments rewired those
+   to `KeyCursor` internally without changing their `seek`/`next`/`get_value` contract. So the
+   batched engine reads segments with no code of its own.
+2. **Cardinality estimates.** `count_batch` calls the same `TriplePattern::estimate_count` the row
+   path uses, and that is where segments added `* segment::estimate_scale()`. The batched engine's
+   "one estimate per distinct key instead of per row" therefore inherits the segment scale for free.
+
+One thing worth naming because it *could* have been a conflict and is not: `estimate_scale()` is a
+single process-wide constant, so it multiplies every index's estimate by the same 256. Proposer
+selection compares estimates against each other, so a uniform factor cannot change which pattern
+proposes. Segments do not perturb the plan. (The scale is nonetheless wrong per index — AVE keeps
+1122 keys where EAV keeps 224 — so absolute estimates are off by ~5x on AVE. It did not change any
+result here, but it is a real limitation inherited from the source branch.)
+
+## New test
+
+Neither source branch tests this pairing. The vectorized equivalence test writes datoms straight to
+an in-memory slate with `db.put`, bypassing the indexer, so it never sees a segment; the segment
+equivalence test goes through `db.query`, which picks whichever engine the env selects, so it never
+compares the two engines against each other.
+
+`segment::tests::equivalence::batched_and_row_engines_agree_over_segments` (src/segment.rs) closes
+the gap: load the 60-vertex graph with retractions and card-one updates at segment size 256, then
+run all 11 queries at two bases through `GenericJoinEngine` and `BatchedJoinEngine` directly and
+compare `variables` and `rows` exactly, including row order. 22 comparisons, all equal. `run(size)`
+was split into `load(size)` + `run(size)` to share the fixture.
+
