+| out_degree | 51.9 | 43.8 | 22.3 | 15.6 | 1.19x | 2.33x | **3.34x** | 2.76x |
+| in_degree_top | 51.6 | 43.1 | 18.9 | 14.8 | 1.20x | 2.73x | **3.49x** | 3.27x |
+| neighbors_of_42 | 1.72 | 1.47 | 0.11 | 0.18 | 1.18x | 15.4x | 9.6x | 18.1x |
+| weight_filter | 4.57 | 3.34 | 2.42 | 1.39 | 1.37x | 1.89x | **3.28x** | 2.58x |
+| weight_sum | 4.58 | 3.34 | 1.90 | 1.34 | 1.37x | 2.41x | **3.42x** | 3.31x |
+| heavy_neighbors | 84.2 | 57.6 | 42.3 | 25.4 | 1.46x | 1.99x | **3.32x** | 2.91x |
+| label_lookup | 0.05 | 0.02 | 0.03 | 0.06 | - | - | - | - |
+
+`three_hop_count` gets six passes instead of three (see below); every other row is the three-pass
+median. `label_lookup` is a 20-50 µs query — it measures process noise, not the database.
+
+Best-of-all-passes minima, all-on versus off, as a sanity check on the medians: triangles 2.64x,
+two_hop 2.59x, out_degree 4.18x, in_degree_top 4.21x, weight_filter 5.22x, weight_sum 5.16x,
+heavy_neighbors 3.65x, neighbors_of_42 24x. The minima are more favourable than the medians
+throughout, which is what a loaded machine does to an experiment.
+
