+## Interactions found
+
+**1. On eight of ten queries the gains do not just compose, they beat the product of the singles.**
+Compare the last two columns: out_degree 3.34x actual against 2.76x predicted, weight_filter 3.28x
+against 2.58x, heavy_neighbors 3.32x against 2.91x, two_hop_count 2.64x against 2.26x. Only
+triangles (2.58x vs 2.56x) and weight_sum (3.42x vs 3.31x) land on the prediction.
+
+The reading is that each feature was partly hidden behind the other. With one key per datom, the
+batched engine still pays an LSM lookup per datom, so removing row allocations exposes less than
+it should — batched alone is only 1.19x on out_degree. With rows still allocated per output row,
+making the scan cheap only shifts the bottleneck — segments alone is 2.33x. Turn both off and the
+query is bounded by whichever is worse at that moment; turn both on and neither is there. That is
+the shape a genuine composition takes, and it is the main result.
+
+**2. `three_hop_count` is the exception, and segments are the reason.** Segments alone do nothing
+to it: 0.98x on six passes, with per-pass off/segments ratios of 0.66, 0.95, 1.33, 0.93, 0.94, 1.08
+— straddling 1.0. The batched engine alone is the biggest single win in the whole experiment,
+3.75x. Together they are 3.09x, i.e. *worse than batched alone*, and per-pass batched/all ratios of
+0.59, 0.84, 0.97, 0.50, 1.07, 1.14 say the penalty is real in four passes of six and reversed in
+two. Median penalty about 20%, spread far too wide for that number to be trusted.
+
+Why segments do not help this query is not mysterious: the vectorized branch profiled triangles at
+~55% malloc/memcpy and ~28% storage iteration, and three_hop has by far the largest intermediate
+result, so the row-layout cost dominates completely. Segments only attack the 28%.
+
+Why segments would *cost* something on top of the batched engine is a hypothesis, not a measured
+fact: `decode_segment` hands out `Bytes` slices of the shared segment value, so every extension
+value the batched engine pushes into its pool holds a reference to a ~9 KB buffer and costs an
+atomic refcount pair. At one key per datom each value is its own small allocation that goes away
+when the level does. On the query with the largest pool, that could plausibly outweigh the cheaper
+seeks. Confirming it needs a profile of the all-on arm, which this experiment did not do.
+
+**3. The columnar regressions the vectorized branch reported are gone, but so is one of its wins on
+small queries.** `neighbors_of_42` was +26% median on the vectorized branch's own A/B; here batched
+alone is 1.18x *faster*, so that regression did not reproduce. But segments alone take it to 15.4x
+(0.11 ms) and adding the batched engine takes it back to 9.6x (0.18 ms). Twenty-one rows out of a
+point lookup is not enough work to pay for the batched engine's setup, and once segments have made
+the lookup nearly free, that setup is the whole query. Same story on `label_lookup`. Both are
+sub-millisecond and should be read as "the batched engine has a fixed cost that only matters when
+there is nothing else to do".
+
+**4. No correctness interaction at all.** Row counts identical in every arm, all 647 tests pass in
+both configurations, and the new cross-engine-over-segments test is exact on rows *and* row order.
+The two features touch genuinely disjoint concerns — one owns the layout of intermediate results,
+the other owns the layout of stored keys — and the `Index` trait is the seam that kept them apart.
+
+## Verdict
+
+The stack is worth having. All on is 2.6x-3.5x on every query that does real work, against a
+baseline where the better single feature would have given 1.5x-2.7x. The combination is more than
+the sum of its parts on eight of ten queries, and the merge cost was one import block.
+
+Three caveats, in order of how much they should bother anyone integrating this:
+
+- **`three_hop_count` is unresolved.** The heaviest query is the one where segments contribute
+  nothing and may cost ~20%. Both source branches already flagged three_hop as their weakest
+  measurement, and this experiment did not fix that — it ran six passes on a loaded machine and got
+  a spread of 0.50 to 1.14. Anyone landing both features should profile the all-on arm on a quiet
+  machine before believing any number in that row.
+- **The `Bytes`-into-segment-buffer question is worth answering before, not after.** If the
+  hypothesis in interaction 2 is right, it argues for the columnar sink the vectorized branch listed
+  as its top missing piece, and possibly for copying short datom values out of the segment buffer
+  rather than slicing it.
+- **Everything each branch said it was missing is still missing.** No bounded batches, no columnar
+  sink, `relation`/`function`/`not`/`or` still fall back to the row engine wholesale, and the
+  duplicated `validate` arms in `triple.rs` are now duplicated across a merge as well. On the
+  segment side the CDC tx-eid filter is still the one genuinely new obligation, write amplification
+  under random single-datom writes is still unmeasured, and `estimate_scale` is still one
+  process-wide constant standing in for a per-index, per-database number.
+
+## Reproducing
+
+```sh
+export PATH="$HOME/.rustup/toolchains/1.95.0-aarch64-apple-darwin/bin:$PATH"
+export VERTICES=2000 EDGE_PROB=0.01 RUNS=5
+# one arm:
+TRIPLOX_BATCHED_JOIN=1 TRIPLOX_SEGMENT_SIZE=256 BENCH_OUT=all.json \
+  cargo bench --bench datalog_bench
+```
+
+Per-arm JSON (median of three passes, per-pass medians kept in each file) is in the session
+scratchpad as `results/combo-vec-seg-{off,batched,segments,all}.json`; the raw per-pass files are
+in `results/combo/`.
